course: politics
lesson: 21
title: Climate: hot summers and hot arguments
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Weather is what the sky does today; climate is the usual weather of a place over thirty years or more. Scientists widely report that the whole world's average temperature has been rising, mostly because people burn coal, oil and gas.
explain: Picture a bath with the hot tap dripping: one splash can still feel cold, but the water slowly gets warmer. That is how a warming world can still have a freezing week in Chicago and a record 40 degrees in England.
explain: Most of the arguing is not about whether it is getting warmer but about what to do: how fast to change cars, power stations and farms, who pays, and what happens to people whose jobs depend on oil, gas and coal.
explain: Each side has its best case. Some say act fast, because waiting makes it worse and costlier; others say go carefully, because jobs, bills and reliable power matter too. Many say a bit of both.
explain: Words from the scenes: «bare» (London) = lots, «innit» = isn't it; «mid» (US kids) = so-so, «bussin'» = really good, «fr» = for real; «eejit» (Ireland) = idiot; «chur» (New Zealand) = thanks or cool, «roast» = make fun of; «arvo» (Australia) = afternoon, «yeah nah» = no; «bunk off» (England) = skip school.
fact: In July 2022 the UK recorded over 40 degrees Celsius for the first time ever, news reports said.
fact: Weather is what happens today; climate is the usual weather of a place over thirty years or more, so one cold day does not cancel a warming century.
fact: Scientists widely report that the planet's average temperature has been rising, mostly because burning coal, oil and gas puts warming gases into the air.
fact: Hotter, drier years make bushfire seasons longer and fiercer, scientists said, and fire experts say clearing dry undergrowth matters too; both can be true at once.
fact: Electric cars run on batteries and give off no exhaust gases on the road, but they can cost more to buy, and charging can be hard on long trips or when towing.
fact: Coal, oil and gas, called fossil fuels, give many people jobs and heat homes, and they are also the main source of the warming gases.
fact: In 2015 almost every country signed the Paris Agreement, promising to try to keep warming well under 2 degrees and ideally 1.5; news reports say the world is not yet on track.
fact: Net zero means putting no more warming gas into the air than is taken out; Ireland, the UK, Canada, Australia and New Zealand have aimed for it by about 2050.
fact: Cows burp methane, a strong warming gas; New Zealand planned to charge farmers for it, then delayed the plan after farmers objected, it was reported.
fact: In 2022 two protesters threw tomato soup at Van Gogh's «Sunflowers» in London; the painting was behind glass and was not damaged, news reports said.
fact: Greta Thunberg began a school strike outside Sweden's parliament in 2018, and millions of young people joined climate strikes, it was reported.
fact: Solar panels make power only when the sun shines, so countries are building big batteries and arguing about how fast to close coal and gas power stations.
exercise: What did news reports say happened in the UK in July 2022? | It went over 40 degrees for the first time ever | It snowed heavily in the middle of summer, all over England | It had its coldest July ever recorded | Every river in England froze for a week
exercise: What is the difference between weather and climate? | Weather is today; climate is the usual weather over many years | Weather is in summer; climate is in winter | Weather is in towns; climate is only at the poles | They are two words for exactly the same thing
exercise: Mateo's grandpa sees snow in Chicago and says «So much for global warming!» What is the problem with that? | One cold day says nothing about the world's average over many years | Snow in Chicago proves the whole planet is cooling | Global warming means it can never snow again | Chicago is not counted in the world's average
exercise: What do scientists widely report is the main cause of the world getting warmer? | Burning coal, oil and gas | The Sun moving closer to the Earth | Volcanoes erupting on the Moon | A hole in the ozone layer letting heat in
exercise: Aisha's teacher blames a hotter climate for the bushfires and her uncle blames dead leaves nobody cleared. What do experts say? | Both matter: hot dry years and dry fuel left on the ground | Only the leaves matter; hot, dry years make no difference at all to fires | Only the weather matters, never the land | Bushfires are only ever started by lightning
exercise: Why do some people prefer electric cars? | They give off no exhaust gases on the road | They never need any electricity at all | They are always cheaper to buy than petrol cars | They can drive for a year without charging
exercise: Why does Noah's dad still like his petrol pickup truck? | It suits long trips and towing, and he already owns it | Petrol trucks give off no warming gases at all, so they are cleaner than electric cars | Electric cars are banned in his country | Petrol is made fresh from sunlight every day
exercise: Why did Kai's uncle go quiet when his cousin said «oil is killing the planet»? | His job and his town depend on oil | He thinks oil is made from sunflowers | He has never heard of climate change | Oil workers are not allowed to talk at all
exercise: What is the Paris Agreement? | A 2015 promise by almost every country to fight climate change | A deal between France and Britain about building a new tower in Paris | A football league between European cities | A law that bans all cars in France
exercise: What does «net zero» mean? | Putting out no more warming gas than is taken back in | Never using any electricity again, in any home, school or factory | Having zero cars left in the whole country | Planting exactly zero new trees each year
exercise: Why are cows part of the climate argument in New Zealand? | Cows burp methane, a strong warming gas | Cows drink all the water from the rivers | Cows' bells make the air warmer | Cows eat the trees that cool the planet
exercise: What happened to the Van Gogh painting the protesters threw soup at? | It was behind glass and was not damaged | It was ruined by the soup and had to be thrown away | It was sold to another museum to pay for the cleaning | It was painted over by the museum's own artists
exercise: What did Greta Thunberg do in 2018? | She went on school strike outside Sweden's parliament | She became Sweden's prime minister at the age of fifteen | She invented the first electric car | She sailed to the South Pole alone
exercise: Grandad says solar panels cannot keep the lights on at night. What is true? | Solar works only in daylight, so power must be stored or come from elsewhere | Solar panels work best at midnight | Solar panels keep sunlight stored inside the glass, so they work all night just as well | Night-time power always comes from the Moon
exercise: What is most of the climate argument really about? | What to do, how fast, and who pays | Whether the Sun is really hot or only looks hot | Whether weather really exists or is just made up | Whether coal is black or grey when it comes out of the ground
test: A girl in London says: «it snowed bare last week, so global warming's a lie, innit». What is the best reply? | One snowy week doesn't change the world's average over many years | Right, a week of snow proves the planet is cooling | Global warming means it can't snow anywhere | Snow only falls when there is no climate
test: An American kid says: «my cousin says EVs are mid and gas trucks are bussin', who's right fr?» Which answer is fair? | Each has pluses and minuses; it depends on cost, trips and pollution | Electric cars suit every family perfectly, whatever trips they make and whatever they cost | Petrol trucks give off no warming gas | Nobody anywhere disagrees about cars
test: What would happen if every country kept its Paris promises? | Warming would be smaller than if the promises were broken | The world would quickly get colder than it was in the Ice Age | Nothing at all would ever change | Weather would stop happening
test: Ethan's grandad in Brisbane grumbles: «bloody solar's useless after dark, mate. Who keeps the lights on then? Coal.» What is the fair reply? | At night solar needs batteries or other power, and people argue how fast to switch | Solar panels work just as well at night | Coal power gives off no warming gas | Batteries can never store electricity
test: A cartoon shows a cow burping a black storm cloud that sets a town on fire at once. What is the true version? | Cow burps add methane, an invisible warming gas, slowly over years | Cow burps are thick black smoke | One burp can heat a whole town at once and start fires straight away | Cows breathe out cold air that cools the planet
test: A boy in Dublin says: «Finn, ya eejit, net zero means no cars at all, sure it does.» Is he right? | No, it means warming gas put out is balanced by gas taken back in | Yes, every car must be scrapped by tomorrow | It means the temperature in the whole country must be kept at zero degrees | It means nobody may use electricity
test: Under the soup video someone writes: «those soup people are clowns, the painting's toast». What was reported? | The painting was behind glass and was not damaged | The painting was destroyed completely and could never be shown again | The soup was really paint remover, so the colours came off | The painting was in Paris, not London, so the video was fake
test: Priya says: «Mum, Greta skipped school, so I can bunk off on Fridays too, yeah?» Which statement is fair? | People disagree: some say young people should protest, others that school comes first | Everyone agrees that all children must strike on Fridays instead of going to school | Skipping school is the law in Sweden | Greta never went to school at all
test: At the barbecue in Alberta the cousin snaps: «oil's killing the planet, end of.» What is the fairest reply? | Oil warms the planet, and it also gives families jobs, so people argue about the change | Oil has nothing at all to do with warming, so the cousin is simply wrong and should stop | Oil workers want the planet to warm | Oil is made fresh every day, so it never runs out
test: A kid in New Zealand says: «chur, but why'd everyone roast Aroha about cow burps?» What is the kind way to see it? | Her family's farm feeds people; the issue is about farming, not about her | Farmers are to blame for all the world's weather, so Aroha deserved to be roasted | Cows don't burp, so the lesson was wrong | Only New Zealand has climate change
test: Aisha's uncle in Sydney says: «heaps of smoke this arvo, sky's gone orange, it's 'cos nobody cleared the leaves, yeah nah?» What is the fair answer? | Dry fuel matters, and hotter, drier years make fires worse too | Only clearing matters; the weather plays no part | The sky turns orange because of the Moon | Bushfires only happen in winter
test: What would it mean if, after a record hot day, it was cold all next week? | It would still be weather; climate is the average over many years | It would prove that the climate was cooling fast, all over the world | It would mean the record was fake | It would mean summer was cancelled
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established distinction between weather and climate. | Weather denotes conditions on a given day, whereas climate denotes the prevailing conditions of a place over thirty years or more | Weather denotes conditions in summer, whereas climate denotes conditions in winter | Climate denotes conditions on a single day, whereas weather denotes the conditions prevailing over a century or more | The two terms are wholly synonymous in scientific usage
exam: Identify the principal cause, as widely reported by scientists, of the rise in the Earth's average temperature. | The combustion of coal, oil and gas | A reduction in the distance between the Earth and the Sun | The influence of volcanic activity upon the Moon | A depletion of the Earth's supply of rainfall
exam: State the commitment undertaken by the signatories to the Paris Agreement of 2015. | To endeavour to hold warming well below two degrees, and ideally to one and a half | To abolish every petrol vehicle and every power station in every member state within a single year | To relocate all power stations to the city of Paris | To guarantee that no snow shall fall in any member state
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term «net zero»? | A condition in which emissions of warming gas do not exceed the quantity removed from the atmosphere | A condition in which no electricity whatsoever is consumed | A condition in which the national temperature is maintained at zero degrees | A condition in which no trees whatsoever are planted
